On Nov 23, 7:34 am, Kent Paul Dolan <xanth...@well.com> wrote:
(on Boots of Stepguarding)
> These sound like something that would prolong the
> life of my low level characters nicely.
>
> However, I'm worried about unexpected consequences
> when used "in the real world of NetHack play".

Of course, everyone who adds items to the game should think hard about
those consequences, and even though I've thought a bit about it it's
impossible to tell without lots of people actually trying them out.
But then, the Dev Team themselves are known to sometimes introduce
first and balance later.

> Wouldn't the behavior as specified have chances for
> some pretty nasty interactions when playing on a
> remote server with irregular response times and some
> keystroke lossage?
>
> I can see wasting several moves before realizing
> that the problem isn't network lag or a lost
> keystroke, but instead is the boot's operation.

Ah, good thinking.  I think the answer is to print a message saying
"For some reason, your feet won't let you move there!"

> Meanwhile, the crowd of monsters following close
> behind gain ground on the player.
>
> Especially for an unspoiled player and unidentified
> boots, this interaction seems like it could be more
> likely lethal than helpful.

Well there's lot of stuff in Nethack like that, they're all part of
Nethack's distinctive game feel.  This relies of particular
circumstances so the chances of death are fairly low (although still
significant).

> Perhaps a prompt "really move <direction>? [yn]"
> would short-circuit this issue, both allowing the
> player to override the boot's decision, warning the
> player more vividly that something weird is just
> ahead, and preventing loss of extra turns
> until the player satisfys the
> prompt[*].

That would make cursed boots less of a problem.  (And we can't make it
so that cursed boots diable the prompt, because it's standard Nethack
behavior that the only additional drawback of cursed equipment is it's
not being removable.)

Good feedback overall, thanks.

- John H.


